WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.604
Open Observability and read the collection
timestamp before interpreting the charts.

00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:11.075
Check service availability, queue pressure
and recent activity together. These are

00:00:11.075 --> 00:00:16.413
synthetic metrics in this tutorial. A quiet
graph can mean low activity, a stale

00:00:16.413 --> 00:00:21.350
collector or a missing signal; it does not
automatically mean good health.

00:00:22.156 --> 00:00:27.519
Inspect the service health details and
compare them with Readiness. Separate an

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:33.086
unavailable endpoint from an unhealthy
dependency or a model error. Latency values

00:00:33.086 --> 00:00:38.042
describe the measured window, not a
permanent service guarantee. Look for

00:00:38.042 --> 00:00:42.456
repeated changes rather than drawing a
conclusion from one point.

00:00:43.262 --> 00:00:48.956
Explore the dashboard links for Grafana,
metrics and traces. Access uses your local

00:00:48.956 --> 00:00:54.170
monitoring configuration. Check units,
legends, time ranges and missing-data

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:59.658
indicators before comparing values. A
service being up does not prove that every

00:00:59.658 --> 00:01:03.912
panel is current or that an external alert
has been delivered.

00:01:04.718 --> 00:01:10.799
Open the architecture view to understand how
requests, tools, memory and observability

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:16.456
connect. Its animated flows explain the
design; they are not live packet traces.

00:01:16.456 --> 00:01:22.325
Correlate an investigation with actual
timestamps and recorded results, then return

00:01:22.325 --> 00:01:26.568
to the relevant service or analysis to
inspect the evidence.
